<p>European Union leaders meeting in Brussels on Thursday failed to persuade Hungarian Prime Minister Viktor Orbán to drop his opposition to a €90 billion loan package for Ukraine, leaving the bloc without the unanimity it says is urgently needed to fund Kyiv’s war effort. The dispute centered on Hungary’s demand to restore oil flows through the Druzhba pipeline after damage and supply disruptions linked to fighting and strikes. Hungary and Slovakia say the interruptions have hurt their energy security. Even with the veto in place, the other 25 EU countries issued a joint statement backing the loan and calling for a first disbursement by early April, underscoring how broad support for Ukraine remains as leaders weigh ways around Budapest’s resistance. Officials and diplomats quoted across several reports said tensions at the summit were unusually sharp. Some leaders accused Orbán of using the issue ahead of Hungary’s April 12 election, while EU foreign policy chief Kaja Kallas said the bloc has options to bypass the veto if governments are willing to act.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/w49yMDp7DgAALfx4P88TkasDbt_mRljuyiZFHJRikJIlpC5KcSH26cJgoce3C4zOfQScvckMbKlmB3vtxbjnkzJSxf6PV48rdzS8T-vg-T_Bzamjp3ktbgY_vtVe6e68-S93qqdnWWF6taRGEROuWuqq-1X6FT64KbnmcdMuBqXNuDouEX_BBvcin19ahQwWUetam1okExk' alt='Prime Minister Viktor Orban of Hungary in Budapest on Sunday. His disputes with President Volodymyr Zelensky of Ukraine have become a focal point of Hungary’s coming election.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Election pressure: Diplomats told Politico and The New York Times that several EU leaders viewed Orbán’s stance through the lens of Hungary’s April 12 election, with his Fidesz party trailing in polls and the Ukraine dispute turning into a domestic campaign issue.</li><li>Slovakia backs Hungary: Slovak Prime Minister Robert Fico supported Hungary’s objections, saying Slovakia is paying the price for losing discounted Russian fuel as the pipeline dispute drags on.</li><li>Possible workarounds: EU foreign policy chief Kaja Kallas said the bloc has legal and political tools to move ahead despite Hungary’s veto, but warned that leaders would need political courage to use them.</li><li>Voting rights: Politico reported that some EU officials and diplomats are discussing tougher options after Hungary’s election, including Article 7 proceedings that could suspend Budapest’s voting rights, though leaders agreed to no such step on Thursday.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Hungary: Orbán argued that his veto is legal and tied any change to the restoration of oil supplies through Druzhba, saying Hungary should not approve money for Kyiv while its own energy deliveries remain disrupted. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-no-deal-90-billion-ukraine-loan-viktor-orban-european-council/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>European Council leadership: European Council President António Costa sharply criticized Orbán during the summit, calling his conduct unacceptable and a breach of the cooperative norms that underpin EU decision-making. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-no-deal-90-billion-ukraine-loan-viktor-orban-european-council/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Ukraine supporters in EU: The 25 other EU leaders publicly endorsed the loan and pushed for the first payment by the beginning of April, signaling that they want support for Ukraine to continue even without Hungarian consent. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-no-deal-90-billion-ukraine-loan-viktor-orban-european-council/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Slovakia: Prime Minister Robert Fico aligned with Budapest and said Slovakia is bearing the cost of losing cheaper Russian oil, framing his position around national energy prices rather than EU institutional procedure. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-no-deal-90-billion-ukraine-loan-viktor-orban-european-council/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Kaja Kallas: The EU foreign policy chief said alternative paths exist to overcome the veto, framing the obstacle less as a lack of tools than as a test of political will among member states. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/eu-has-tools-to-bypass-hungarys-ukraine-loan-veto-but-must-show-courage-kallas-says'>The Straits Times</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/3/19/eu-leaders-slam-hungarys-orban-for-blocking-ukraine-aid-package'>EU leaders slam Hungary’s Orban for blocking Ukraine aid package</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://abcnews.com/International/wireStory/eu-leaders-blast-viktor-orbn-ukraine-loan-veto-131210459'>EU leaders slam Orbán over Ukraine loan veto, accuse election games</a> - abcnews.com</li><li><a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-no-deal-90-billion-ukraine-loan-viktor-orban-european-council/'>EU braced for no deal on €90B Ukraine loan as Orbán refuses to budge</a> - politico.eu</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/635533-eu-hungary-ukraine-loan/'>EU threatens Hungary over Ukraine loan – Politico</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/eu-summit-hungary-holds-ukraine-aid-ransom-over-druzhba-oil/live-76430675'>EU summit: Hungary holds Ukraine aid ransom over Druzhba oil</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/19/world/europe/ukraine-hungary-orban-elections.html'>A Crucial Loan for Ukraine Becomes Election Leverage for Orban</a> - nytimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/europe/eu-leaders-fail-to-convince-hungarys-orban-to-lift-block-on-ukraine-loan'>EU leaders fail to convince Hungary&#x27;s Orban to lift block on Ukraine loan</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/19/hungary-orban-ukraine-loan-veto-eu-summit-europe'>Viktor Orbán refuses to agree to €90bn loan for Ukraine as EU leaders accuse him of betrayal</a> - theguardian.com</li></ul>